{
  "concept": "A digital signature is checked by sending the message and signature together, transforming the signature with Alice's public key, and accepting only if that result matches the received message.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "large heading 'Alice' above the left half",
    "large heading 'Bob' above the right half",
    "long horizontal communication-channel line running from Alice to Bob",
    "label 'communication channel' centered below the horizontal line",
    "incoming message label M' at the far left of the channel",
    "vertical branch from the message line up to Alice's signing box",
    "right-pointing arrow into Alice's signing box",
    "gray signing box labeled S_A",
    "label 'sign' above the S_A box",
    "output arrow from S_A to the right",
    "label sigma = S_A(M') above Alice's output line",
    "downward arrow from Alice's signature output line to the communication channel",
    "transmitted pair label (M', sigma) above the channel between Alice and Bob",
    "vertical branch on Bob's side carrying sigma upward from the received pair",
    "label sigma near the upper Bob branch",
    "right-pointing arrow into Bob's public-key verification box",
    "gray verification box labeled P_A",
    "label 'verify' above the P_A box",
    "output line from P_A to the right and then downward into the comparison box",
    "lower Bob channel segment carrying the received M'",
    "label M' above the lower Bob message branch",
    "upward arrow from the lower message branch into the comparison box",
    "small gray comparison box labeled =?",
    "right-pointing arrow from the comparison box",
    "accept label at the right of the comparison arrow",
    "reject label or red rejection indication used only in tampered mode",
    "interactive highlight overlay for the active step arrows",
    "interactive highlight overlay for the sigma path through P_A",
    "interactive highlight overlay for the direct M' path into the comparison box"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "exchangePhase",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Step through signature check",
      "states": [
        "start",
        "sign",
        "transmit",
        "verify",
        "compare"
      ],
      "default": "start",
      "teaches": "The learner sees the exchange as an ordered process: Alice signs the message, sends both values, Bob transforms the signature with Alice's public key, and only then compares it with the received message."
    },
    {
      "id": "transmissionMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Transmission",
      "options": [
        "Untampered",
        "Tamper M'",
        "Tamper sigma"
      ],
      "default": "Untampered",
      "teaches": "Verification accepts only when the received message and received signature are a matching pair; changing either value during transmission makes the final comparison fail."
    },
    {
      "id": "bobBranchHover",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Hovering over Bob's two incoming branches separates their roles: sigma must first be transformed by P_A, while M' goes directly into the comparison box as the reference value."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Start with the message",
      "narration": "The message M' enters Alice's side on the communication channel. A copy branches upward because Alice signs the message before it is sent.",
      "state": {
        "exchangePhase": "start",
        "transmissionMode": "Untampered"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Alice signs",
      "narration": "Alice applies her signing operation S_A to M', producing the signature sigma = S_A(M'). The signature is not the message itself; it is a value derived from the message using Alice's signing key.",
      "state": {
        "exchangePhase": "sign",
        "transmissionMode": "Untampered"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Send the pair",
      "narration": "The channel carries the pair (M', sigma) to Bob. Bob needs both parts: the message being claimed and the signature that is supposed to authenticate it.",
      "state": {
        "exchangePhase": "transmit",
        "transmissionMode": "Untampered"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Bob verifies the signature",
      "narration": "Bob sends sigma through P_A, Alice's public verification operation. This produces the value that should match the received message if the signature is valid.",
      "state": {
        "exchangePhase": "verify",
        "transmissionMode": "Untampered"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare and accept",
      "narration": "The transformed signature and the received M' meet at the =? box. In the untampered case they match, so Bob accepts.",
      "state": {
        "exchangePhase": "compare",
        "transmissionMode": "Untampered"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Tamper with the transmission",
      "narration": "If the message or the signature is changed on the channel, the two inputs to the =? box no longer match. Bob rejects because the signature no longer verifies the received message.",
      "state": {
        "exchangePhase": "compare",
        "transmissionMode": "Tamper M'"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Bob does not merely check that a signature exists; he checks that P_A(sigma) equals the exact message M' he received.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the original as a clean black-and-gray block diagram on a white background. Alice occupies the left half and Bob the right half, with a single long communication-channel line spanning the bottom. Use gray rectangular boxes for S_A, P_A, and =?, with serif math labels. In each step, highlight the active arrows in a bright color such as blue or orange while inactive arrows remain black. In untampered mode, the comparison box should glow green and the outgoing label should read accept; in either tampered mode, visibly mark the changed M' or sigma in red on the channel, make the comparison box glow red, and show reject instead of accept. Hovering over Bob's sigma branch should brighten the path up through P_A and down into =?, while hovering over the M' branch should brighten the direct lower path into =?."
}

